type Item {
    name: String!
    folder: String
    sizeBytes: Long
    mimeType: String
    dataSourceName: String!
    dataSourceType: DataSourceType!
    dateCreated: String!
    dateModified: String!
    downloadLink: String
    rawBase64Content: String
    previewLink: String
    # Detected faces with their identity labels, in face-index order. Resolved lazily — only
    # queries that ask for it pay for the lookup. The detectedface attribute lists the names in a
    # photo; this says which face carries which, keyed by the same index the crop URL uses.
    faces: [ItemFace!]!
}

type ItemFace {
    # Stable 0-based position of the face within its item; the key the crop URL addresses
    faceIndex: Int!
    # Identity label, or null when the face has no name (recognition off, not yet drained, or
    # skipped). An unlabeled face renders as a plain crop with no caption and no rename option.
    label: String
}

type Folder {
    name: String!
    folder: String
    dataSourceName: String!
    dataSourceType: DataSourceType!
}

type ItemAttribute {
    key: String!
    value: String!
}

type SearchResult {
    searchConditions: [SearchCondition!]
    name: String
    folder: String
    searchString: String
    offset: Int!
    howmany: Int!
    totalHits: Int!
    sortDirection: SortDirection!
    items: [Item!]!
}

type SearchCondition {
    name: String!
    operation: SearchOperation!
    value: String!
}

type Facet {
    key: String!
    values: [FacetValue!]!
}

type FacetValue {
    value: String!
    count: Int!
}

"""
One filter on a single item attribute.

Values are matched against the attributes produced by treatments, keyed by name
(`content`, `name`, `path`, `content-type`, EXIF and ICC keys, and so on). Use
`availableItemAttributeKeys` to discover which keys exist.

Two field classes behave differently, and this is the one distinction a caller has to
learn:

* **Metadata fields** match **characters**. `istribut` finds `distributed` in a filename.
* **Content fields** (`content`, `content-<N>`, `ocr-content*`) match **whole words**.
  `istribut` finds nothing in document text; there is no way to ask for a mid-token
  substring there.

**Known limit — large documents.** Extracted text beyond roughly 500KB is stored under
numbered keys (`content-0`, `content-1`, … and likewise `ocr-content-<N>`) rather than
under `content`. A condition naming `content` therefore does not see such a document at
all: `LIKE` matches none of them and `NOT_LIKE` matches all of them. `searchString` is
unaffected — it searches every key. Prefer `searchString` for document text unless you
specifically need to filter one key.
"""
input SearchConditionInput {
    "Attribute key to filter on, e.g. `name`, `path`, `content`, `content-type`."
    name: String!
    operation: SearchOperation!
    """
    The value to match. For `LIKE` and `NOT_LIKE` this is parsed: whitespace separates
    terms, and a term wrapped in double quotes is a phrase. All terms must be satisfied
    by **one value of the named key** — `name LIKE "annual report"` means both words in
    one `name`, never one word in `name` and the other in `path`.
    """
    value: String!
}

"""
How a `SearchConditionInput` value is compared.

`LIKE` and `NOT_LIKE` are exact complements of one another: every item satisfies exactly
one of them. `LESS` and `GREATER` are **not** complements — an item whose value is equal
to the given one, or which does not carry the key at all, satisfies neither.

**Negation is over the item, not the value.** `NOT_EQUAL` and `NOT_LIKE` compile to
`NOT EXISTS`, so they are satisfied by an item that simply does not carry the key — not
only by one that carries it with a non-matching value.
"""
enum SearchOperation {
    "Item has a value for this key that sorts before the given one (string comparison)."
    LESS
    "Item has a value for this key that sorts after the given one (string comparison)."
    GREATER
    "Item has a value for this key exactly equal to the given one."
    EQUAL
    "Item has no value for this key exactly equal to the given one, including having no such key."
    NOT_EQUAL
    """
    One value of this key contains **all** of these terms.

    Unquoted terms may appear in any order, need not be adjacent, and — on a metadata
    field — may be mid-word: `name LIKE Microsoft Word` matches
    `Microsoft Office Word`, `Microsoft® Word for Microsoft 365` and
    `Microsoft_Word_-_report.pdf`.

    Quote a term to require it **contiguous and in that order**: `name LIKE "Microsoft
    Word"` matches only the literal phrase. Quoting is the only way to ask for adjacency,
    and on a content field it is the expensive option — cost grows with how many
    documents contain the words at all, and a common phrase can take seconds.
    """
    LIKE
    """
    No value of this key satisfies `LIKE`, including the item having no such key at all.

    Note the direction: because quoting narrows `LIKE`, it **widens** `NOT_LIKE`.
    `NOT_LIKE "annual report"` excludes only items with that exact phrase, while
    `NOT_LIKE annual report` excludes every item holding both words anywhere in one value.
    """
    NOT_LIKE
}

enum SortDirection {
    ASC
    DESC
}

